WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.190
MARK MIRCHANDANI: You can't
have jobs without companies.

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:03.820
In our last video,
we set everything

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:07.260
to start using the Google Cloud
Talent Solution Job Search API

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.540
and started by
creating a company.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.880
Now, let's dive deeper
into creating and updating

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.130
companies, as well as
checking out their properties.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:16.884
[UPBEAT MUSIC]

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:21.790
Here's the Python code we
used to create the company.

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:23.220
As you can see,
it's pretty short.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.350
But let's quickly go
through what we're doing,

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:28.090
since a lot of it will
apply to the API as a whole.

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:30.785
We'll be using version 3 of
the API for all of our code.

00:00:30.785 --> 00:00:32.910
Don't forget to check out
the documentation for any

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:34.650
of the latest updates.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.560
Everything above this line is
setting up the client library.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.630
But we're also setting
a global variable

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:41.970
with the format
of projects slash

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:43.890
and then the ID of your project.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:46.380
This is important, because all
the companies you deal with,

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.660
and then later, jobs,
will have this project

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:50.352
as the parent they belong to.

00:00:50.352 --> 00:00:51.810
It's also easy to
see this when you

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:53.893
look at the name of a
created company, which we'll

00:00:53.893 --> 00:00:55.920
look at when we run the code.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.810
Inside our function, we'll set
up the company to be created,

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:01.980
which gets passed in, and
make the actual request.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:03.420
The client library
makes it clear

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:07.110
that we're creating a company
with projects.companies.create.

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:09.150
And then we pass
that project ID along

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:10.740
with our company properties.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:14.040
This code also prints out the
resulting company details.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:15.870
After some error
checking in our function,

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:17.730
we'll go back and set
the required fields

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.470
and call create_company.

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:21.810
Right now, we're only using
two fields, display name

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:23.160
and external ID.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:25.950
These are the only required
fields for creating a company.

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:28.360
The display name is exactly
what it sounds like.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:30.330
It's the name that
displays for the company.

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:32.730
External ID is your
identifier to help

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:35.550
you track the company with
whatever your job system has.

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:38.460
You can put whatever reasonable
string makes sense here or just

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:40.120
put the same thing
as a display name,

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:42.150
if you don't have a
current system identifier,

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.340
or if you're a staffing
agency and don't

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.943
want to reveal the hiring
company's information.

00:01:46.943 --> 00:01:48.610
With just these, we'll
create a company.

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:50.140
So let's run the code.

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:52.620
Here's a printout of the
details of the company.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:54.330
We can see the
fields we passed in

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:56.430
as well as the name
of the company, which

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.676
is generated on a
create and serves

00:01:58.676 --> 00:02:02.610
as a unique identifier for this
company in the job search API.

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:04.410
We'll use this anytime
we need to reference

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:07.170
the company in the API,
such as updating it

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:09.250
or when we create jobs.

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:12.300
You can see that it follows
the same format from before--

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:16.590
projects, slash, your project
ID, slash, companies, slash,

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:18.360
and then the unique
company name.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:20.370
Great, we can now
create companies

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:22.753
and use their unique
names as identifiers.

00:02:22.753 --> 00:02:24.420
Now, let's go back
and take a quick look

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:27.310
at that list_companies code
from the previous video.

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:29.100
Thanks to the client
libraries, this code

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.500
looks almost identical
to the create code.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.020
Inside of our function,
we'll do companies.list

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.570
instead of
companies.create, but still

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:38.762
pass in the same project ID.

00:02:38.762 --> 00:02:40.470
Once we make the call,
we'll just quickly

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:42.270
check if there are
any companies returned

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:45.745
and list their display names
along with their unique names.

00:02:45.745 --> 00:02:47.370
With this short
snippet, we can quickly

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:50.430
see what companies we've
set up with the API.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.500
Let's run the code
and see some output.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:54.540
Here's two companies
I made earlier.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.580
Pretty straightforward,
but what happens when

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:58.500
you need to update a company?

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.490
We'll do that by patching it.

00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:01.800
Here's our update code.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.270
Hopefully the structure
looks pretty familiar.

00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:06.660
We'll be calling patch
to update the company.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:09.240
And we'll pass along the
unique name, as well as

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:12.780
a full company object with all
the fields we want to update.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:15.120
The project ID isn't needed
here, because that's already

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:17.430
included in the company name.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:19.350
Here's where we set
the name and the fields

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:21.280
in the updated company object--

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:23.487
in this case, a
new display name.

00:03:23.487 --> 00:03:25.320
It's important to note
that you can't change

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.630
the external ID or
the automatically

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:29.790
generated name for any company.

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:32.610
When updating a company, you
should include every property

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:34.590
in the passed in
object, even those that

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:36.150
aren't going to be updated.

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:38.010
Otherwise, any
missing properties

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:40.170
would get removed
from the company.

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:42.090
To avoid any confusion
there, we actually

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:44.420
recommend providing
an update mask

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:47.310
where you specify only the
fields you want to update.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.260
This is a string
that you can pass in

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:52.830
with a comma separated list of
fields that you want to update.

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:55.500
Here's some modified code
that shows setting an update

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.930
mask to update the
display name along

00:03:57.930 --> 00:03:59.940
with the size of the company.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:03.180
Size is an enum that we'll
talk about in just a second.

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:05.640
When we make this API
request, only the fields

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:09.150
explicitly listed in the update
mask will actually be updated.

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:12.250
And other fields will
just be unchanged.

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:16.329
Now, let's look at size and
other properties of a company.

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:18.029
The more information
a company has,

00:04:18.029 --> 00:04:20.490
the better the experience
will be for job seekers.

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:22.830
As we just saw, size
is an enum that you

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:24.540
can set to represent
the amount of people

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:26.010
that work at the company.

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:27.270
There's a few options here.

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:29.400
And if you're using the
Python Client libraries,

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:31.380
you can just pass
in the string value.

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:33.213
Here's some other
properties you'll probably

00:04:33.213 --> 00:04:34.140
want to include.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.180
HeadquartersAddress, which
is the string address

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:37.770
of the company's headquarters.

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:40.110
The API will
geolocate the address

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:43.230
and try to fill in more specific
details in a read-only property

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:45.060
named derivedinfo.

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:47.460
Jobs will have
their own addresses.

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:49.650
HiringAgency is a
Boolean if this company

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:51.960
post jobs for other employers.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:55.200
Eeotext is a string that lets
you include an equal employment

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.170
opportunity legal disclaimer
for all of the jobs associated

00:04:58.170 --> 00:04:59.670
with this company.

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:02.400
WebsiteUri is a string
for the company's website.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:05.340
And careerSiteUri is a string
specifically for the company's

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:06.720
career site.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:09.510
ImageUri is a string
for the company's logo.

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:12.330
You can also delete companies
if you don't need them anymore.

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:14.100
The delete operation
is pretty simple,

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:16.770
and you just pass along the
company's name to delete it,

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:18.552
exactly as you might expect.

00:05:18.552 --> 00:05:20.260
It's important to note
that a delete will

00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:23.718
fail if the company has any
open jobs associated with it.

00:05:23.718 --> 00:05:25.510
Now that you can work
with companies, let's

00:05:25.510 --> 00:05:27.570
start talking about jobs.

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:29.180
Then again, we've
covered a lot today.

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:31.032
So let's talk about
jobs in the next video.

00:05:31.032 --> 00:05:33.490
Check out the documentation if
you're eager to get started.

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:35.597
And let us know if you
have any questions.

00:05:35.597 --> 00:05:36.430
Thanks for watching.

00:05:36.430 --> 00:05:38.200
And remember, when
looking for talent,

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:40.090
it's OK to keep your
head in the Cloud.

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:43.140
[SMOOTH MUSIC]

